# D51 / `todo/t0718` — `unify`'s smart-pointer coercion is keyed on the TYPED
# builtin IDENTITY (`DefInfo.builtin_kind`), never on the definition's NAME.
#
# THE ELEVENTH CHANGED CELL, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT IS A MESSAGE RATHER THAN A
# VERDICT. `Mutex(5)` with a generic `struct Mutex[T]` in scope fails to infer
# `T` from the constructor — a PRE-EXISTING, NAME-INDEPENDENT gap, which is why
# the rejection itself does not move. What moves is the BLAME:
#
#   pre-fix   error[E_TypeMismatch]: expected `int`,        found `Mutex`
#   post-fix  error[E_TypeMismatch]: expected `Mutex[int]`, found `Mutex`
#
# Pre-fix, the coercion arm answered `true` on the USER's `Mutex` and unwrapped
# the annotation `Mutex[int]` to `int` before reporting, so the diagnostic named
# a type the author never wrote. Post-fix it names the annotation, and matches
# the name-neutral control (`struct Wrap[T]` + `Wrap(5)`) EXACTLY — which is the
# proof that the message is no longer a function of the spelling.
#
# ⚠ THE PROBE THAT FIRST DISMISSED THIS CELL HAD NO `struct Mutex[T]` AT ALL —
# it measured the BUILTIN path, where `Mutex[int] m = Mutex(5)` is rc 0 on both
# sides, against a control that DID declare its struct. Not a matched pair. The
# builtin observation is true and is exactly what made the mismatch invisible.
#
# The forbidden string is the pre-fix spelling, so this row fails if the arm
# ever starts unwrapping a user type's annotation again — a regression a bare
# exit-code sweep is structurally blind to.
#
# Asserted by `coercion_identity_user_mutex_ctor_blames_the_annotation`.

struct Mutex[T]:
    T value

void main():
    Mutex[int] m = Mutex(5)
    print(1)
